+
+**Why this block.** The derivation campaign's verdict table (#8093) had one row left to compute ("Menus — neighbour-generated supports"); its Born half is closed by PR #8137; the support half is computed here.
+
+## Result, in one paragraph
+Under the unsoldered reading (independent internal rotations), a record with no recorded neighbour is uniform on the sphere (the landed note), a record with one recorded neighbour of value `q` has a support invariant under the rotations about `q`, so a finite support is contained in `{q, −q}` (M1), and two non-collinear recorded values have trivial stabilizer, so the covariant supports are exactly the frame-attached assignments `F(q_1, q_2) S_0(q_1·q_2)` — no site-independent finite menu is covariant (M2). Under the soldered reading a site-independent finite menu is covariant iff it is a union of orbits of the cube's rotation group on the sphere, whose sizes are `6` (axes, stabilizer order 4), `8` (diagonals, 3), `12` (edge directions, 2) and `24` (all other points, 1); the six-axis menu of blocks 01–17 is the unique smallest nontrivial covariant menu (M3). Consequently, under the unsoldered reading with finite supports, every window in which each record after the first sees exactly one recorded neighbour carries a two-valued field on the uniformly random seed's axis, and in general the alphabet is generated by the formation from the seeds (M4); and the Born overlap `(1 + s·q)/2` is deterministic copying on the antipodal pair, so a nontrivial Born statistic needs a support beyond it (M5). The clause reading is recorded (soldered: a supplied orbit union; unsoldered: a generated alphabet), not adopted.
+
